I wrote The Beat Before the Bells for the demonstration. It comes from a classic least common multiple problem. If three bells ring every 3, 4, and 5 beats, when do they first ring together? Beat 60.

The story adds a twist. Each player prepares exactly one beat before ringing. So if the strikes line up at 60, their preparations line up at 59. Iona can find the moment to act by listening for the scrape, the creak, and the breath together.

That tells us something about positioning and coordination. The conductor listens for the chord because it confirms that everyone arrived. Iona listens one beat earlier because that’s when she can still change what happens. The boy, the woman, and her mother each keep their own rhythm. None of them has to count to 59. Iona just has to recognize what their separate cues mean when they arrive together.

The question you can carry into another story is what happens just before everybody notices?